      <h2>1. Start with the job your website needs to do</h2>
      <p>A focused service business may only need one clear page: explain the offer, show relevant information, answer common questions, and make contact easy. A business with several services may need individual pages so visitors can find the information that matters to them.</p>
      <p>Write down your primary visitor, the question they arrive with, and the action you want them to take. That gives you a better starting point than choosing a page count in isolation.</p>
      <h2>2. Separate the build from the running costs</h2>
      <p>A website budget has at least two parts: the initial project and the cost of keeping the site online. Additional services, subscriptions, and content work may sit outside both.</p>
      <div class="table-scroll"><table><caption>Illustrative Rumi Works package costs</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Proposed scope</th><th scope="col">Initial build</th><th scope="col">Annual renewal after year one</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">1 page, up to 6 sections</th><td>RM990</td><td>RM499</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Up to 5 pages</th><td>RM2,490</td><td>RM699</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">Up to 8 pages + standard CMS</th><td>RM4,490</td><td>RM999</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <p>First-year hosting and SSL are included in these proposals. Domain registration and renewal are separate. Any applicable taxes, third-party fees, and additional work should be confirmed in the quotation.</p>
      <p>For example, the proposed five-page package totals <strong>RM3,189 across the initial build and one subsequent annual renewal</strong> (RM2,490 + RM699), before domain costs, optional care, additional work, third-party fees, and any applicable taxes. This is an illustration, not a binding two-year quote.</p>
      <h2>3. Decide who will prepare the content</h2>
      <p>Good layouts still need accurate information. Plan who will supply the service descriptions, company details, photographs, logos, and answers to customer questions.</p>
      <p>If you need copywriting, photography, translations, or brand design, include those in the brief. They should not be assumed to be part of a website build unless the quote says so. Having approved content ready can also reduce avoidable review cycles.</p>
      <h2>4. Pay for features you will actually use</h2>
      <p>A CMS can be useful if you regularly update agreed types of content. If you only change a sentence occasionally, a simpler website and a small edit arrangement may be enough.</p>
      <p>“Standard CMS” should specify the platform, editable fields, user access, and any licence costs. It should not be confused with a bespoke dashboard, an ecommerce system, or a customer portal.</p>
      <p>Online payments, bookings, member accounts, integrations, and data migration all introduce additional work. Ask what they cost to build, what they cost to run, and who will be responsible for them.</p>
      <h2>5. Make maintenance a deliberate choice</h2>
      <p>The proposed Rumi Works website care option is <strong>RM149 per month for up to 30 minutes of small content edits</strong>. Unused time does not roll over. Twelve monthly payments would total RM1,788.</p>
      <p>This is optional and separate from annual hosting renewal. It does not include new pages, a redesign, new features, or custom-software support. Decide whether that arrangement matches how frequently your content changes.</p>
      <h2>6. Ask these questions before accepting a quote</h2>
      <ul><li>Exactly which pages, sections, and features are included?</li><li>Who supplies the copy, images, and domain?</li><li>Which review rounds and acceptance criteria are included?</li><li>What happens if the scope changes?</li><li>What are the renewal and third-party costs?</li><li>Who holds the domain, hosting, and account access?</li><li>What is the handover process, and what support follows it?</li><li>What content and approvals are needed before the schedule starts?</li></ul>
      <h2>A clear scope is the best starting point</h2>
      <p>A lower initial price is not automatically the better option. Compare the same deliverables, ownership arrangements, responsibilities, and ongoing costs. A straightforward brief makes those comparisons much easier.</p>
